east misconstrued smiley emojis are  frequently embellished  with popular interpretation aids such as  hearts or  tears or dominant upturned or downturned  mouths  so while yes hair is one factor other  things like clothing and accessories can  inform perception  given how fluid and dynamic language and  gender presentation can be  it's not inconceivable that regardless  of haircut shirt collar  accessories other clues gender inclusive  emoji could be  repurposed in some other manner like  prince  princess emperor head of state  and because i work at google i really  wanted to understand  how google search interprets images as  it relates to gender  a researcher at google had been  exploring what happens when you apply a  makeup classifier to an image  and what features change in that image  so i took  the image she generated and just put  them through google image search results  so you see this this image of a  prototypical we might call a man  right uh google image sent back  possible related search gentlemen i was  like okay google image search gets it  they get that this is a picture of  a man so ran the next image through  and it changed it to boy super  interesting okay so what happens when we  run the next image through  oh it shot that boy again okay no change  there  got a little excited then the fourth  image also  reads back boy maybe maybe this  experiment will get me nowhere but i ran  the fifth one through  and it changed a girl and the next one  also became girl the last one  returned models with bleached brows so  we're getting somewhere interesting as  it relates to what the makeup classifier  is changing  but also that there is the spectrum of  interpretation not just in gender but  also in age  so tackling the first phase of using  gender inclusive designs  for code points which do not specify  gender is pretty clear  support existing code points that do not  have  gender so this was google's  emoji font set in 2018  and this is here it is in 2019 fully  compliant  to unicode guidelines so now when you  text someone  i feel so relaxed  you don't have to worry you can feel  relaxed  when we launched these the reception was  enormously positive  people finally feeling like they felt  like they didn't have to choose between  two genders anytime they just wanted to  simply express  some joy  most of the gender inclusive emoji  rolled out in 2019 although some rolled  out in 2020 as well and we're seeing  much wider adoption  there's been rapid development as major  vendors such as apple  roll out their designs and twitter as  well  the experience around selecting these  emojis seems to be a work in progress  apple has chosen to simply add the third  option alongside male  and female designs google has opted for  one design to be the default  asking people to opt into their gender  this also makes it  room so much easier to just browse your  keyboard  for the right emoji because there's  there's less redundancy  but now comes the hard part what to do  with the 12  explicitly gendered emoji okay  man in tuxedo this is fairly simple  we make it a person in tuxedo we add  support for women in tuxedo and man  tuxedo  now we have jane bond jesse bond and  james bond  that seem logic can apply to woman  person  and man in bail but what do we do  about mrs claus and santa claus  we get mixed claws now i know you're  thinking  there's no such thing as mix clause but  there's also no such thing as santa  claus  now the breastfeeding emoji did stump  the group a bit  do we simply change the existing design  for this code point  to remove it from the explicitly  gendered list  and then we can if so desire add  additional sequences for men  and women well the general consensus was  people did not feel comfortable changing  the woman breastfeeding emoji to be more  inclusive  and since we don't use sex signifiers to  denote gender  the presentation in our designs we're  really unable to settle on a reasonable  fallback  and opted instead to create an entirely  new emoji  man woman and person feeding baby the  logic being simply that  since the only emoji that depicts anyone  with child care was breastfeeding  there just really weren't any emoji that  really expressed nurturing your child  in my opinion though parenthood is  already representable  with a tornado and a pile of poop  and you know people one of the things i  do try to think about  is how this emoji is being used and how  it will be used and so finally we do  have an appropriate father's day emoji  other emoji like bearded person  are clearly designed in a way that's  more masculine  moving forward bearded person will be  shown in a more  inclusive manner but what to do  about bearded women and bearded man  beards have long been indiscriminate of  gender throughout history  and truth be told this isn't really  about women with beards or  men with beards it's more of a question  of do people with long hair have beards  and the answer is yes right anyone you  watch eurovision  there's a great number of men and women  with long hair and beards in the world  and many that are very cultural relevant  which is why these resolutions can be  complex we can't simply change the  design of existing emoji  anytime culture changes so we have to  anticipate not just where we are now  but where we will be going the flamenco  dancer  she's iconic she's really become  something emblematic  of some something so much more than  simply dancing  but if you really go back and think  about iconography  look at the man bathroom symbol why does  this mean man  and not just person because someone  added a skirt to create a woman design  why would they do that because what  they've effectively done  is reinforced that what would have been  a perfectly acceptable default gender  inclusive symbol  for person is now a man which reinforces  our man-centered society  i don't know what the blobs were they  were just good little friends  and this was the original dancer emoji  in 2013.  it wasn't a woman it wasn't a man it was  a child it wasn't an animal  it was just a dancer  and you know 2015 phones get fancier  details get added and now we have these  fairly sexualized dancers and  in their specificity and detail  they have alienated a great deal of  other kinds of dance what about african  dance polynesian dancing or boogie  woogie  and in 2019 we see something that  is really fun but when you're thinking  about encoding emoji and putting  broad concepts that cover the concept of  dancing  not specifically flamenco dancing paying  attention to those details is really  important  that i digress like what do we do what  do we do about woman flamenco dancer  and man disco dancer we don't have  something that just conveys  the concept of dancing we are not going  to change  the existing code points this is not a  path forward we want to move forward  with  so what if we added a third type of  dance for the gender inclusive like  um this you know be provocative here's a  breakdancer  but it seems appealing right but is it  future proof like will we still be happy  with this  gender inclusive type of dance  five years from now two years from now  10 years from now  will this create more problems in the  future or less  in my mind if we get gender inclusive  break dancing emoji  why don't we have it for feed for women  and for men as well  so another possibility is to just simply  design  male and person counterpoints the  flamenco dancer  and women and gender inclusive character  points to the disco dancer  but do we really need this many flamenco  dancers on our keyboard  and this many disco dancers probably not  another emoji that is being discussed  is the pregnant woman emoji one sex  doesn't really dictate the capacity to  care  for in the home or at work or in the  market right  so if you look towards sex equality law  that aims to combat harmful sex  stereotypes it has a lot of parallels  with how we're unsexing